						" (every 9.1 option, colors and --dired; plus "
						<code> "dir"
						" and "
						<code> "vdir"
						"), "
						<code> "date"
						" (with all of gnulib's parse-datetime, "
						<code> "--debug"
						" included), "
						<code> "grep"
						" (GNU grep 3.8: binary files, -r with include/exclude, colors) "
						<code> "sed"
						" (GNU sed 4.9, with its error positions), "
						<code> "find"
						" (findutils 4.9.0, -printf and -exec + included), "
						<code> "hexdump"
						" and "
						<code> "hd"
						" (util-linux 2.38.1 with its -e format language), "
						<code> "rev"
						", "
						<code> "strings"
						" (binutils 2.40), "
						<code> "groups"
						", "
						<code> "which"
						", "
						<code> "sync"
						" "
						<code> "free"
						" and "
						<code> "kill"
						" (procps 4.0.2), and "
						<code> "hostname"
						" (Debian 3.23, with dnsdomainname/domainname, over a port of glibc's resolver path) are now ports of their coreutils sources, each checked against GNU on hundreds to thousands of random cases."
				<article className="card card-core">
					<h3> "dd, join, checksums and encodings, ported from coreutils"
					<p>
						<code> "dd"
						" follows GNU coreutils 9.1's "
						<code> "dd.c"
						": every conversion including the EBCDIC tables, block and unblock, swab and sparse; every flag ("
						<code> "fullblock"
						", "
						<code> "count_bytes"
						", "
						<code> "direct"
						", "
						<code> "nocache"
						", ...); GNU's number syntax with "
						<code> "kB"
						"/"
						<code> "KiB"
						", "
						<code> "B"
						" and "
						<code> "x"
						"; "
						<code> "bs="
						" passing each read through while "
						<code> "ibs"
						"/"
						<code> "obs"
						" regroup; the statistics lines with gnulib's rounding, "
						<code> "status=progress"
						", and reports on SIGUSR1 and Ctrl-C. "
						<code> "join"
						" ports "
						<code> "join.c"
						", down to the obsolete "
						<code> "-j1 ARG"
						" operand rules, on top of a port of glibc's "
						<code> "getopt_long"
						". The nine checksum tools ("
						<code> "md5sum"
						", "
						<code> "sha1sum"
						" to "
						<code> "sha512sum"
						", "
						<code> "b2sum"
						", "
						<code> "cksum"
						", "
						<code> "sum"
						") are one port of "
						<code> "digest.c"
						" over nebox's own streaming MD5, SHA-1, SHA-2, BLAKE2b, SM3 and CRC code, with "
						<code> "--tag"
						", "
						<code> "-z"
						", escaped names and GNU's check-file parser. "
						<code> "base64"
						", "
						<code> "base32"
						" and "
						<code> "basenc"
						" port "
						<code> "basenc.c"
						" with gnulib's decoders, all eight encodings included. Comparisons against GNU on 754 dd, 1,596 join, 1,989 checksum and 2,588 encoding cases found no difference in output, diagnostics, exit status or files left behind."
				<article className="card card-core">
					<h3> "patch, read from GNU's source"
					<p>
						<code> "patch"
						" places hunks with GNU patch 2.7.6's own search (offsets outward, then fuzz), detects reversed patches and asks GNU's questions, rebuilds rejects the way GNU does, and backs files up when a patch did not match exactly. A fuzzer feeds both programs random diffs in three formats against shifted, edited and already-patched files: 2,000 cases with identical output, status, result, reject and backup files."
				<article className="card card-core">
					<h3> "diff, step for step with GNU"
					<p>
						<code> "diff"
						" ports GNU diffutils 3.8's analysis: identical ends trimmed on bytes with horizon lines, lines of one file with no match in the other discarded first, the middle-snake divide and conquer with its cost cut-off, and boundary shifting. The edit scripts are GNU's: a fuzzer compares both on random files for twelve option sets, 24,000 comparisons without a difference, and 40,000-line files with heavy changes match too."
			<h2 className="section"> "Known limits"
			<p className="lead"> "Stated rather than worked around. The full list is in the repository's README."
			<ul>
				<li>
					"No multibyte locales anywhere: strings are bytes, collation is byte order, "
					<code> "-i"
					" folds ASCII."
				<li>
					<code> "sh"
					" is a POSIX shell checked against dash by 444 cases: the full grammar, expansions, redirections, traps and the special and regular builtins. Interactive use, job control included, is tested on a pseudo-terminal (24 sessions); "
					<code> "fc"
					", vi mode and "
					<code> "ulimit"
					" are not implemented. Where POSIX and dash disagree, nebox follows POSIX and the case is pinned."
				<li>
					<code> "awk"
					" is POSIX awk checked against GNU Awk 5.2.1 "
					<code> "--posix"
					"; GNU extensions such as "
					<code> "gensub"
					", "
					<code> "strftime"
					" and "
					<code> "BEGINFILE"
					" are not there, and "
					<code> "RS"
					" uses only its first character."
				<li>
					<code> "date"
					" follows GNU date's formatting rules in local time (TZif files, POSIX TZ strings, "
					<code> "TZDIR"
					"), and "
					<code> "-d"
					" reads a subset of gnulib's parse-datetime (ISO and US dates, times with zones and am/pm, "
					<code> "@SECONDS"
					", "
					<code> "yesterday"
					", weekdays, "
					<code> "3 days ago"
					", "
					<code> "next month"
					", GNU's quirk that "
					<code> "+1"
					" after a time is a zone); month names, ordinal words, "
					<code> "-I"
					", "
					<code> "--rfc-3339"
					", "
					<code> "-f"
					" and setting the clock are not there."
				<li>
					<code> "ls"
					" lays out columns like GNU ls 9.1 ("
					<code> "-C"
					", "
					<code> "-x"
					", "
					<code> "-m"
					", "
					<code> "-w"
					", "
					<code> "TABSIZE"
					") but has no quoting styles, colour, "
					<code> "-L"
					"/"
					<code> "-H"
					" or "
					<code> "BLOCK_SIZE"
					". "
					<code> "du"
					" and "
					<code> "find"
					" walk directories in sorted name order where GNU uses directory order."
				<li>
					<code> "sed"
					" has "
					<code> "-z"
					", "
					<code> "M"
					" and "
					<code> "e"
					" but no "
					<code> "--posix"
					", "
					<code> "-u"
					", "
					<code> "v"
					" or the "
					<code> "\\d \\o \\x \\c"
					" escapes; "
					<code> "grep"
					" has GNU context lines, "
					<code> "-z -Z -L -f -s"
					" and "
					<code> "-o -w"
					" as GNU prints them, but no long options or binary-file detection; "
					<code> "sort"
					" has the GNU option surface ("
					<code> "-k"
					" modifiers, "
					<code> "-h"
					", "
					<code> "-V"
					") but no "
					<code> "-M"
					"; "
					<code> "xargs"
					" has no "
					<code> "-P"
					". "
					<code> "seq"
					" matches GNU seq 9.1 for floats, "
					<code> "-f"
					", "
					<code> "-w"
					" and integers past 2"
					<sup> "64"
					"."
				<li>
					<code> "tail -f"
					" polls once a second. "
					<code> "timeout"
					" polls the child every 2 ms instead of using signals."
				<li>
					"Names for ids come from reading "
					<code> "/etc/passwd"
					" and "
					<code> "/etc/group"
					" directly (nelibc's new "
					<code> "getpwuid"
					" reads the same files)."
				<li>
					<code> "diff"
					" has normal, context, unified and ed output and "
					<code> "-i -b -w -B -a -N -q -s -r"
					", but no long options, "
					<code> "-p"
					", "
					<code> "-y"
					", "
					<code> "-D"
					" or "
					<code> "-I"
					"."
			<h2 className="section"> "Build"
			<pre>
				<code> "git clone https://github.com/Spaceghost/nelibc\nmake -C nelibc lib-standalone-zig\ngit clone https://github.com/Spaceghost/nebox\nmake -C nebox NELIBC=../nelibc        # build/nebox\nmake -C nebox NELIBC=../nelibc test   # the conformance suite"
			<p className="note">
				"Builds and tests are recorded in a Debian 12 container with Nelua "
				<code> "3c29419"
				", Zig 0.17.0 and GNU coreutils as the reference. The repository's CI runs the same suite on every push."
			<div className="actions">
				<a className="button" html:href="https://github.com/Spaceghost/nebox"> "Repository"
				" "
				<a className="button button-ghost" html:href="https://github.com/Spaceghost/nebox/blob/main/tests/run.sh"> "Conformance suite"

export def footerLinks
	<div className="footer-links">
		<a html:href="https://github.com/Spaceghost/nebox"> "Spaceghost/nebox"
